   "src/bistro/web/bookings/routes.py",
   "src/bistro/web/bookings/routes__test.py"
  ],
  "hits": [
   "The route module imports the BookingRepository port and `guest_page` calls `bookings.bookings_for_guest(name)` directly. Web code must call commands and queries and never import a port. The guest lookup should be a query (for example GetGuestBookings) that `main.py` builds with the repository and passes to the routes."
  ],
  "patch": "cases/vm03.patch",
  "set": "V-must"
 },
 "vm04": {
  "changed": [
   "src/bistro/web/bookings/routes.py",
   "src/bistro/web/bookings/routes__test.py"
  ],
  "hits": [
   "The walk_in route decides which slot is 'now', which tables are taken, and which table to give (smallest that fits). Those are booking rules that belong in a command's handle(). Because they sit in the route, the time is also read with datetime.now() instead of coming from a Clock port, so tests cannot fix the date."
  ],
  "patch": "cases/vm04.patch",
  "set": "V-must"
 },
 "vm05": {
  "changed": [
   "src/bistro/web/bookings/routes.py",
   "src/bistro/web/bookings/routes__test.py"
  ],
  "hits": [
   "The opening-hours and Monday rules are proven only through route tests. The rule has no test in the command's `__test.py` against the in-memory adapter."
  ],
  "patch": "cases/vm05.patch",
  "set": "V-must"
 },
 "vm06": {
  "changed": [
   "src/bistro/booking/models/booking.py"
  ],
  "hits": [
   "The model enforces a booking rule (party size 1-8) by raising ValueError. A party of 0, or 9 at a table with more than 8 seats, passes CreateBooking.handle's checks, reaches the Booking(...) call at line 57 and raises, so the guest gets an unhandled exception rather than a returned failure.",
   "Deciding whether a party size is allowed is policy and belongs in CreateBooking.handle, which today only checks party against table.seats. The model now enforces a limit that the command neither checks nor tests, so no command-level test covers it."
  ],
  "patch": "cases/vm06.patch",
  "set": "V-must"
 },
 "vm07": {
  "changed": [
   "src/bistro/booking/ports/booking_repository/in_memory_booking_repository.py",
   "src/bistro/booking/ports/booking_repository/sqlite_booking_repository.py"
  ],
  "hits": [
   "The in-memory adapter decides whether a guest may book (one booking per guest per day) and refuses by raising ValueError. That policy belongs in the command's handle(), and a guest-caused refusal should be a returned failure, not an exception. Nothing in CreateBooking catches the ValueError, so a second booking by the same guest on a day would crash the request.",
   "The SQLite adapter's save() does a read (`_select`) and then a write (insert). It also enforces the booking policy and raises ValueError for something a guest can cause. The command should read the guest's bookings that day, return a failure such as a new member of CreateBookingFailure, and only then call save()."
  ],
  "patch": "cases/vm07.patch",
  "set": "V-must"
 },
 "vm08": {
  "changed": [
   "src/bistro/booking/queries/next_free_slot.py",
   "src/bistro/booking/queries/next_free_slot__test.py"
  ],
  "hits": [
   "The query `NextFreeSlot.handle` saves a new Booking through `self.bookings.save(...)` and consumes an id from the generator. A query must only read, so a second call with the same input gives a different result and the store changes. This should be a command."
  ],
  "patch": "cases/vm08.patch",
  "set": "V-must"
 },
 "vm09": {
  "changed": [
   "src/bistro/booking/commands/cancel_booking.py",
   "src/bistro/booking/commands/cancel_booking__test.py",
   "src/bistro/booking/ports/booking_repository/booking_repository.py",
   "src/bistro/booking/ports/booking_repository/booking_repository__test.py",
   "src/bistro/booking/ports/booking_repository/in_memory_booking_repository.py",
   "src/bistro/booking/ports/booking_repository/sqlite_booking_repository.py"
  ],
  "hits": [
   "An unknown booking id is something a guest can cause, but `handle()` raises `BookingNotFoundError` (an Exception subclass) instead of returning a frozen failure dataclass with a `reason` that belongs to a failure union. Callers such as routes would have to catch an exception and can't use the exhaustive failures.py match. The test also asserts the raise."
  ],
  "patch": "cases/vm09.patch",
  "set": "V-must"
 },
 "vm10": {
  "changed": [
   "src/bistro/booking/commands/create_booking.py",
   "src/bistro/booking/commands/create_booking__test.py"
  ],
  "hits": [
   "A blank or over-60-character guest name is something a guest can cause through the form, but `handle()` raises `ValueError` for it. The steering file requires guest-caused failures to be frozen dataclasses with a `reason`, in the failure union and returned. As written, the route does not catch the exception, so the guest gets a 500 instead of a handled status from `failures.py`."
  ],
